// @run
// @log 3
// @log y
// @log 2.5
// AN INFERRED EMPTY `[]` WHOSE ELEMENT A LATER `.push` PINS TO AN ARRAY.
//
// The nested-array sibling of `unions/un-annotated-empty-list-union-push.vl`, and it was the
// last element kind on this path with no producer at all. Two things had to be added, and the
// shape of the hole is exactly the one that file's header describes.
//
// THE ELEMENT HAD NO RENDER. `nodeArrayElemName` carried arms for a scalar, a struct, a map, a
// union, a closure and a nullable closure — and none for a nested ARRAY, so it answered "" and
// `collectLocals` synthesized no annotation at all. The binding then took the i32-list default
// and the array pushed into it stored a ref into an i32 element: a module that CHECKS CLEAN and
// does not instantiate (`expected i32, found (ref $type)`).
//
// AND THE ROW HAD TO EXIST BEFORE THAT SYNTHESIS RUNS. The synthesis postdates `collectA`, so it
// can only RESOLVE a ref-list row, never mint one. `arrLitIsRef` declines an empty literal by
// construction, and the nested arms of `arrLitElemKind` / `arrLitElemName` key off the FIRST
// ELEMENT'S NODE — which an empty literal does not have. So `collectA` interns the row from the
// same query, on the empty literal itself, exactly as it does for the union case.
//
// Both other routes to this shape already ran before this change — the ANNOTATED
// `const outer: i32[][] = []` and the nested LITERAL `[[1, 2, 3]]` — so the machinery was all
// present and only the inferred-empty route was missing. The three leaf kinds below are the
// three the element kind splits into (i32 wrapper 4, string 6, f64 7).

function i32Nest() {
  const outer = []
  const inner = [1, 2, 3]
  outer.push(inner)
  print(outer[0][2])
}

function strNest() {
  const outer = []
  const inner = ["x", "y"]
  outer.push(inner)
  print(outer[0][1])
}

function f64Nest() {
  const outer = []
  const inner = [1.5, 2.5]
  outer.push(inner)
  print(outer[0][1])
}

i32Nest()
strNest()
f64Nest()
